{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries","id":998892,"title":"Questions about emergency surgeries","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-05T14:42:25-07:00","id":998892,"title":"Questions about emergency surgeries","content":"Hi, <br /> <br /> one of the player characters is a surgeon in an emergency room. Therefore I'm trying to figure out how everything works starting from being badly injured and bleeding to death until finally no more medical care is necessary. <br /> <br /> In reality someone who enters the emergency room has still a high chance of dying. Therefore <br /> in my opinion it should happen now and then that a patient does not survive the surgery based on the rolls.<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> Obviously there are three phases where medical dice rolls are needed after some was badly injured and is now close to death:<br /> <br /> 1) First aid / Ambulance <br /> Dexterity + Medicine + Modifiers <br /> One roll per minute. <br /> Extended action. <br /> Success equal to the health are needed.<br /> <br /> 2) Emergency surgery. <br /> Dexterity + Medicine + Modifiers <br /> One roll per minute. <br /> Extended action. <br /> Success equal to the health are needed.<br /> <br /> Sources:<br /> &quot;She can\u2019t recover from that without urgent medical attention and emergency surgery.&quot; <br /> (CofD p. 95)<br /> <br /> Medical care is an extended action, requiring successes equal to the total number of points of damage suffered by the patient. In the field or ER, the dice pool is Dexterity + Medicine, and the interval is one minute. <br /> (CofD p. 95)<br /> <br /> 3) Medical treatment in the hospital (until all aggravated damage is healed). <br /> Intelligence + Medicine + Modifiers<br /> One roll per hour.<br /> Extended action.<br /> Successes needed are depending on the damage type and amount.<br /> <br /> Source:<br /> In long-term hospital care, the pool is Intelligence + Medicine, and the interval is one hour. The number of successes required is five for a lethal wound and 10 for an aggravated one.<br /> (CofD p. 95)<br /> <br /> ---<br /> <br /> And now my problem:<br /> I can't find anything that makes a emergency surgery difficult that is mentioned in the rules. The characters is a surgeon working in an emergency room where he has also all the equipment that he needs. So there is no way he would ever be reduced to a chance die. So failures would only make the surgery take more time ...<br /> <br /> The 1E rule book also mentioned that a normal failure just means no progress:<br /> &quot;Failure: Your character makes no progress in alleviating the patient\u2019s injuries. If the subject is bleeding to death, a wound turns from lethal to aggravated over the course of the wasted minute.&quot;<br /> <br /> Since it is said that you need both, first aid and an emergency surgery, I kind of wonder why they look so similar and exchangeable. Furthermore the &quot;difficult part&quot; seem to be the first aid where someone has only minutes before he bleeds to death. For example a person with seven health dots can't survive for more than seven minutes. In my opinion this is not long enough to get someone to the emergency room. Even with an immediate call and the ambulance starts driving to the location right away. So stopping the bleeding has to happen before. And is therefore the goal of the first aid. <br /> After that the emergency surgery is next. But what could happen? The immediate danger where the character can bleed to death is over, otherwise he wouldn't have made it so far. <br /> <br /> Did I overlook something? I don't think it is very interesting nor realistic if an surgery can only become longer...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":1825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","user":"Elythia","date":". 11-05-2016, 05:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-05T15:09:32-07:00","id":998896,"title":"","content":"First aid and ER are treated as the same action in this system. Either way you're rolling Dex + Medicine to stabilize someone who is bleeding out. The biggest difference is that in the ER the character probably has access to better equipment. The primary risk is just that you might not get enough successes before the patient dies from the bleeding. Although, since it's an extended action, failures don't just do nothing. You have to take a negative Condition to continue, which will most likely apply penalties to subsequent rolls on the extended action, meaning more likelihood of failed rolls. And you have a limited number of rolls you can make. So, it is entirely possible to fail the extended action before achieving the target number of successes, leaving the target to bleed out. And while a &quot;natural&quot; dramatic failure isn't super likely for a well-trained character, the player can always opt to turn a regular failure dramatic and take a Beat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 11-05-2016, 06:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-05T15:25:06-07:00","id":998906,"title":"","content":"Thank you for the quick reply. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I have already considered that they are treated like the same action. But on the other hand the book says both are required:<br /> &quot;She can\u2019t recover from that without urgent medical attention AND emergency surgery.&quot; <br /> (CofD p. 95)<br /> <br /> The second problem is the logic. If you start bleeding to death you can never reach the emergency room in the given amount of time unless you are already in a hospital. <br /> Furthermore it doesn't make sense that the first aid can make an emergency surgery obsolete.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-05T22:30:09-07:00","id":999074,"title":"","content":"I solved this problem in my games by saying that Dramatic Faiulre effect of doctor can pass to patient. You botched your surgery but it's patient that will be Blind from now on.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T01:26:20-07:00","id":999113,"title":"","content":"This game simplifies advanced and complicated actions all the time, but it feels like Mental skills suffer the most from this; combat rolls, pursuit rolls (drive and athletics), and social maneuvering are all fairly complex while the only mental skill with an in-depth system is investigation (unless I'm forgetting something). I've wanted to play a primarily mental focused character for a while but it feels like unless both the ST and player has real-life knowledge about the subject, mental actions will always be reduced to dice rolls. The excitement and intrigue conveyed in TV shows such as House (medicine), MacGyver (crafts) or Numb3rs (academics/investigation) is super hard to bring into a RPG. It feels like I am going off on a tangent, but the discussion here feels worthwhile to elaborate a bit.<br /> <br /> Diagnosing, treatment, surgery, etc, etc are very complicated things (same with other mental skills). How do you build a narrative around mental skills in a way that is as immersing and engaging gameplay as physical or social skills? My experience has often been 'roll attribute + skill' to find an abstracted solution to the problem, but that's not always super satisfying when trying to weave a story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12103-vintervalpen","name":"Vintervalpen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12103_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T01:57:36-07:00","id":999117,"title":"","content":"@Wyrdhamster: <br /> <br /> I like the idea, that the patient gets the condition. But I think it's very unlikely for the player to let that happen on purpose. Especially if someone else would be involved, not only his own character.<br /> <br /> He has Dexterity: 3, Medicine: 3, a specialization in surgery and emergency care (+1) and all the equipment he needs (+3 or +4). So even with a penalty of -5 he would never be reduced to a chance die.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":1825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","user":"Elythia","date":". 11-06-2016, 04:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T01:27:34-08:00","id":999120,"title":"","content":"My understanding of the penalty and bonus system is that only the end result cannot exceed +5/-5. For example, a Demon rolling Cover after partially manifesting their demonic form rolls Wits + Manipulation as well as a +1 for every form power they have not manifested. By default Demons have seven form powers, and there are ways of having more. This usually means a flat 5 bonus to the roll. However, that roll can be penalized by other things - say that someone or a group sees them doing an act who is not aware they are a Demon. This means that the +6 a Demon normally gets for the roll can be reduced down to a -5 if enough people see them.<br /> <br /> Basically, if you have enough external negative factors, you can hit someone with enough penalties to overcome any innate bonuses they get from equipment and specialties. There are also powers and conditions that artificially reduce the dice pool someone has to begin with, if not forcing certain actions into being chance die. If someone has a high enough attribute and skill pool, then it is unlikely they will have a chance die, but it still makes their rolls harder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T04:55:23-08:00","id":999166,"title":"","content":"@taidragon:<br /> Thank you very much for the clarification! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> Having multiple modifiers that can reach from -5 to +5 makes sense. But I don't think that it is correct to use +6 inside the calculation anywhere, because you start adding the bonus dice first. And here a single bonus has a max. of +5. So the + 1 can only be added by another bonus.<br /> ---<br /> <br /> I was thinking about everything and since this is an extended action a chance die would almost always kill the patient or inflict a lot of conditions in the process.. Since I as the storyteller decide the difficulties, reducing the player that far is like telling him there is no good outcome possible. He can just roll to see how bad things are...<br /> <br /> But that's not what I want. I want a fair chance for the player with a small possibility for a bad outcome with his stats and equipment. <br /> <br /> I really like the first situation where you have a limited amount of time. So every time the player rolls the dice it feels important to him, because a failure reduces his chance of success before the time is over. I think that's the kind of tension I want for the emergency surgery, too. Every roll should feel important since a life is at stake here. I think that's also the reason why it is an extended action with one roll per minute. <br /> Normally resetting the dice pool at a failure or offering a condition makes kind of sense, if you are working on a machine or maybe a computer program. No harm is done, it just doesn't work that way. You clean up your code find the mistake and continue. Only time is lost and its kind of frustrating for the character.<br /> But a human is not that simple in my option. &quot;Working&quot; on a human should have some consequences, if you do something wrong. <br /> <br /> At the moment I am thinking about adding one point of bashing damage for every failure during the surgery. Under normal circumstances this would never be a problem. But in this specific situation it would:<br /> The first aid at the original location stops the bleeding. This is usually the case somewhere between 3 and 5 points of aggravated damage. This means the character can usually take only 3 to 1 more points of bashing damage. One point more would be lethal. <br /> And more than one failure would also add a condition to the patient, while failing the surgery would add a condition to the doctor.<br /> In addition everything that happens influences the next step. A patient where the bleeding was stopped very fast has now actually a better chance of survival.<br /> <br /> I know I was asking for an official rule, but it doesn't seem to get me anywhere. So I tried to stay close. Especially because I don't have much experience in the game so far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":1825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","user":"Elythia","date":". 11-06-2016, 08:03 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T08:16:21-08:00","id":999222,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elythia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries?p=999166#post999166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I really like the first situation where you have a limited amount of time. So every time the player rolls the dice it feels important to him, because a failure reduces his chance of success before the time is over. I think that's the kind of tension I want for the emergency surgery, too.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is how stabilizing rolls work. If you don't succeed in enough time, the patient will die from their injuries.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elythia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries?p=999166#post999166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Every roll should feel important since a life is at stake here. I think that's also the reason why it is an extended action with one roll per minute. Normally resetting the dice pool at a failure or offering a condition makes kind of sense, if you are working on a machine or maybe a computer program. No harm is done, it just doesn't work that way. You clean up your code find the mistake and continue. Only time is lost and its kind of frustrating for the character.<br /> But a human is not that simple in my option. &quot;Working&quot; on a human should have some consequences, if you do something wrong.</div> </div> </div> </div>Failure isn't necessarily &quot;doing something wrong&quot;, it's just failing to accomplish what you were intending to do within the time interval. And the consequence of failing is built into the mechanics. You either make it more difficult for yourself to continue or you fail the attempt. Meanwhile every roll you make without reaching the target number of successes is another minute and another wound going from lethal to aggravated.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elythia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries?p=999166#post999166\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">At the moment I am thinking about adding one point of bashing damage for every failure during the surgery. Under normal circumstances this would never be a problem. But in this specific situation it would:<br /> The first aid at the original location stops the bleeding. This is usually the case somewhere between 3 and 5 points of aggravated damage. This means the character can usually take only 3 to 1 more points of bashing damage. One point more would be lethal. <br /> And more than one failure would also add a condition to the patient, while failing the surgery would add a condition to the doctor.<br /> In addition everything that happens influences the next step. A patient where the bleeding was stopped very fast has now actually a better chance of survival.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think maybe you're getting caught up on the term &quot;emergency room.&quot; Stabilizing a dying person is Dexterity + Medicine with a one minute interval and a target number of successes equal to the damage, whether you are in the field or at an emergency room. Further aiding someone in recovering from injury after they have been stabilized is Intelligence + Medicine with a one hour interval and target number dependent on the type of damage you are trying to heal, whether you are in the emergency room or long-term care. If you stabilize someone with first aid and then take them to the ER, the emergency surgeon is making Intelligence + Medicine rolls to reduce the damage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T10:18:37-08:00","id":999290,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries?p=999222#post999222\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Failure isn't necessarily &amp;quot;doing something wrong&amp;quot;, it's just failing to accomplish what you were intending to do within the time interval. And the consequence of failing is built into the mechanics. You either make it more difficult for yourself to continue or you fail the attempt. Meanwhile every roll you make without reaching the target number of successes is another minute and another wound going from lethal to aggravated.<br /> <br /> I think maybe you're getting caught up on the term &amp;quot;emergency room.&amp;quot; Stabilizing a dying person is Dexterity + Medicine with a one minute interval and a target number of successes equal to the damage, whether you are in the field or at an emergency room. Further aiding someone in recovering from injury after they have been stabilized is Intelligence + Medicine with a one hour interval and target number dependent on the type of damage you are trying to heal, whether you are in the emergency room or long-term care. If you stabilize someone with first aid and then take them to the ER, the emergency surgeon is making Intelligence + Medicine rolls to reduce the damage.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think, that might be how it was actually meant to be. ^^<br /> <br /> But as mentioned, this would logically result in all stabilization rolls outside of the hospital. Of course I wouldn't treat every failure like that, but you have also to consider that this is the worst condition a human can be in. He is almost dead. And you can not stop every wound from bleeding with first aid. It just &quot;helps&quot; getting the patient to the ER still alive. He can still die during transportation or in the ER. And that is not so unlikely.<br /> <br /> So in order to get as close to reality as possible and also to make the rolls more interesting for the player of the surgeon, I think this might be a good compromise.<br /> <br /> I agree that I do focus on the ER. Without someone playing a surgeon working in the emergency room I don't think, there is a need to change anything. But since I do have such a player, I want that those rolls feel important and are not a guaranteed success with more or less time. That would be to boring.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-06T12:06:56-08:00","id":999364,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elythia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/998892-questions-about-emergency-surgeries?p=999290#post999290\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think, that might be how it was actually meant to be. ^^<br /> <br /> But as mentioned, this would logically result in all stabilization rolls outside of the hospital. Of course I wouldn't treat every failure like that, but you have also to consider that this is the worst condition a human can be in. He is almost dead. And you can not stop every wound from bleeding with first aid. It just &quot;helps&quot; getting the patient to the ER still alive. He can still die during transportation or in the ER. And that is not so unlikely.<br /> <br /> So in order to get as close to reality as possible and also to make the rolls more interesting for the player of the surgeon, I think this might be a good compromise.<br /> <br /> I agree that I do focus on the ER. Without someone playing a surgeon working in the emergency room I don't think, there is a need to change anything. But since I do have such a player, I want that those rolls feel important and are not a guaranteed success with more or less time. That would be to boring.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, in this particular case you want to focus on the ER because it is where your action will happen. It is precisely where the game usually simplify things because most groups won't have a PC working at ER. I think I can come up with some simple rules for your entertainment.<br /> <br /> 1st- Effects of Damage: A character gets Fast Dying Condition. The only default source of it is to get the Health Track full of Lethal. At the ST's discretion the character can get it from a source of Aggravated damage without filling the other boxes, and maybe other sources can cause it. With Fast Dying, the character takes 1 Bashing Damage each minute until the Condition is Resolved. Usually it means upgrading Lethal to Aggravated, given context. The Condition is Resolved (and the Beat taken) only with 1st Aid, but the Resolution also causes the Slow Dying Condition.<br /> <br /> 2nd- Effects of First Resolution: Resolving the Fast Dying Condition downgrades it to the Slow Dying Condition, though the former doesn't need to be the only source of the latter. The thing is to make the Slow Dying Condition the actual target of procedures on ER. The Condition works similarly to Fast Dying, but the damage comes per hour, and it is Resolved with ER Procedure. Slow Dying, once Resolved, doesn't cause any further Condition by itself, but the Procedures can result in other Conditions.<br /> <br /> 3rd- Effects of Equipment: There is where you'll fine-tune the entire thing. As a rule of thumb, be conservative with bonuses here. The default procedures, both for 1st Aid and ER, shouldn't have bonuses. They are Default, after all. The right tools here aren't making things easy, but possible. The effects of lesser tools can vary, from dice penalty to more time between rolls, or anything else you thing appropriate for a given set of tools. Only the most high-end tools or exceptional contexts should give any bonuses. Or, instead of bonuses, they could let some other characters to actually help your rolls. The default staff support shouldn't give any advantage, it is default. But situations or equipment that let professional characters to help in associated stuff, like diagnosis, with exceptional proficiency, could let the professional roll to provide bonuses. At the end, the exact effects have to be fine-tuned by you.<br /> <br /> 4th- 1st Aid Roll: It is an extended Dex + Med roll, with also means it gets the character's base dice-pool as a limit for how much rolls she gets. It doesn't interrupt the effects of Fast Dying and it is 1 minute per roll, so the target's wounds are effectively worsening at each roll that don't Resolve the Condition, even a successful one. As a change, I suggest for your case to make the Target Number into 3+1 per Aggravated on the Dying character. Most of the times a patient gets to ER it will be already on Slow Dying Condition, but on a slightly worse situation than when taken on field or arrived at the hospital, because it isn't likely that the 1st Aid had all the needed successes at once. If it was more than an hour from field to ER, it will also have worsened. As per above suggestions, the tools used on field aren't proper for ER Procedures, and if the character is forced to make them this way, it should be at penalty.<br /> <br /> 5th- ER Procedures Roll: Even though I know Dex is crucial for a surgeon, I would suggest to make it into a Composure + Medicine Roll instead. What makes it worse for the hands of the doctor here isn't low natural Dex, but the need to keep calm under pressure and ignore distractions. Either way, just keep it similar to 1st Aid, but with hours instead of minutes. Take into account all the possible complications until the patient got into ER to decide how much Agg the surgeon will have to deal with. Now you have the character actively racing against time, every roll meaning an actual change in the situation instead of just adding successes to an almost unavoidable complete recovery.<br /> <br /> 6th- Complications, Consequences and Strategy: You'll probably find everything coming down to &quot;moah dmg&quot; becoming a little dull. Also, this system can be too harsh sometimes, taking out the fun of truly dramatic surgeries. For that, I suggest a relatively simple means of actually reducing the damage taken by the Patient. When there is a failure at the roll, the ST can choose to apply, instead of another point of damage, a Condition to the Patient. This Condition can either make it harder for the 1st Aid or ER rolls, or be a Persistent Condition. Also, if the character doing the roll desperately needs to buy more time, the player can spend 1 success to achieve the same effect, but with a non-Persistent Condition that won't make it harder for future rolls, instead. The actual Condition is at the ST's discretion, though it can be reasonable to ask and listen to suggestions from the player.<br /> <br /> It seems a lot, but boils down to the following:<br /> <br /> TL;DR: The character loses Health each minute until treated at 1st Aid. If successfully treated, takes a Beat and looses Health each hour until treated at ER. If this is achieved, takes a Beat and is alive and ready to recover. Treating at each stage is Extended with target 3+1/Agg, and the roll takes the time until next damage. Equipment shouldn't give bonus easily, because it is more of a requirement. And you can change damage for Conditions during procedure if you need to slow down the worsening.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T08:54:05-08:00","id":1002541,"title":"","content":"Wow, thank you! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> <br /> <br /> I think I will only adjust the difficulty a little bit. If you reduce the bonus dice and increase the difficulty at the end with the new formula, people just keep dying, I think.<br /> I noticed that an average character with Dex. 2 or 3 and Medicine 1 almost always fails at first aid. Or the person barely survives with one box of lethal instead of aggravated damage. And these characters are most of the times the only ones available. So maybe half of the health rounded up should be enough successes to get someone to the hospital. (And I think it makes sense to give a bonus for a first aid kit for external wounds only. It doesn't help with a heart attack for example.) At the hospital you should have around three tries (four boxes) left this way. But with no bonus dice and starting at 0 the new formula seems to harsh again. So maybe the default amount of successes should be applied!?<br /> I think I will need some time to find the right balance. <br /> <br /> But thanks again. I find your ideas really helpful! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Waiting for new Chronicles of Darkness Dice! \ud83e\udd17 \ud83c\udfb2","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1825-elythia","name":"Elythia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1825_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"100"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-13T10:23:19-08:00","id":1002554,"title":"","content":"Be welcome.<br /> <br /> I knew I wouldn't have the same experience as you to set the precise difficulties, so that's why I said on the effects of equipment to fine-tune things. What I would suggest is to not make the target number too different. You can change the basic number I suggested, from 3 to 1, let's say. It should be enough to make a full lethal just 1 success away from stabilizing from Fast to Slow Dying, and them to recovery.<br /> <br /> On the main, though, I suggest to tune it through equipment bonus. Making a list of possible Conditions, maybe during a brainstorm with your players, will also help, because then you'll have a grasp of how much resources you'll have to deal with failures or slow building-up.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}